This error (`Could not choose the best candidate function`) happens when PostgreSQL has multiple functions with the same name but different parameter types (e.g., one expecting a UUID and another expecting a Number/BigInt), and it doesn't know which one to use.

Since your `subject_enrollments` table uses `bigint` for IDs, you need to remove the incorrect `uuid` version of the function and ensure only the `bigint` version exists.

Run this SQL block in your Supabase SQL Editor to fix the conflict:

```sql
-- 1. Drop ALL potential variations of the function to clear the conflict
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS approve_enrollment_request(uuid);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS approve_enrollment_request(bigint);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS approve_enrollment_request(text);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS approve_enrollment_request(integer);

-- 2. Create the single, correct version using BIGINT
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION approve_enrollment_request(enrollment_id bigint)
RETURNS void
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY DEFINER
AS $$
DECLARE
  v_student_id uuid;
  v_subject_id bigint;
  v_teacher_id uuid;
BEGIN
  -- Get current user (teacher)
  v_teacher_id := auth.uid();

  -- Update status and capture IDs for notification
  UPDATE subject_enrollments
  SET status = 'approved'
  WHERE id = enrollment_id
  RETURNING student_id, subject_id INTO v_student_id, v_subject_id;

  -- Verify the update happened
  IF v_student_id IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Enrollment request not found';
  END IF;

  -- Insert notification for the student
  INSERT INTO notifications (
    recipient_id,
    actor_id,
    type,
    related_entity_id,
    related_subject_id
  )
  VALUES (
    v_student_id,
    v_teacher_id,
    'enrollment_approved',
    enrollment_id,
    v_subject_id
  );
END;
$$;

This error occurs because the `enroll_in_subject` database function is trying to insert a notification for the teacher, but the default Row-Level Security (RLS) policies usually prevent a user (the student) from creating records that belong to another user (the teacher).

To fix this, you need to update the `enroll_in_subject` function in your Supabase SQL Editor to be **`SECURITY DEFINER`**. This grants the function the privileges of the database owner, allowing it to bypass RLS checks for the notification insert.

Run this SQL script in your Supabase SQL Editor:

```sql
-- Drop the old function to ensure a clean update
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS enroll_in_subject(text);

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION enroll_in_subject(subject_code_to_enroll text)
RETURNS json
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY DEFINER -- THIS IS THE KEY FIX: Grants permission to insert notification for the teacher
AS $$
DECLARE
  v_subject_id bigint;
  v_teacher_id uuid;
  v_student_id uuid;
  v_existing_status text;
  v_enrollment_id bigint;
BEGIN
  v_student_id := auth.uid();

  -- 1. Find the subject and teacher based on the code
  SELECT id, teacher_id INTO v_subject_id, v_teacher_id
  FROM subjects
  WHERE subject_code = subject_code_to_enroll;

  IF v_subject_id IS NULL THEN
    RETURN json_build_object('success', false, 'message', 'Invalid subject code.');
  END IF;

  -- 2. Check if already enrolled or pending
  SELECT status INTO v_existing_status
  FROM subject_enrollments
  WHERE subject_id = v_subject_id AND student_id = v_student_id;

  IF v_existing_status IS NOT NULL THEN
    IF v_existing_status = 'pending' THEN
      RETURN json_build_object('success', false, 'message', 'You already have a pending request for this subject.');
    ELSIF v_existing_status = 'approved' THEN
      RETURN json_build_object('success', false, 'message', 'You are already enrolled in this subject.');
    ELSE
      RETURN json_build_object('success', false, 'message', 'You have already applied to this subject.');
    END IF;
  END IF;

  -- 3. Insert enrollment request and capture the new ID
  INSERT INTO subject_enrollments (subject_id, student_id, status)
  VALUES (v_subject_id, v_student_id, 'pending')
  RETURNING id INTO v_enrollment_id;

  -- 4. Notify the teacher (This was failing before without SECURITY DEFINER)
  INSERT INTO notifications (
    recipient_id,
    actor_id,
    type,
    related_entity_id,
    related_subject_id
  )
  VALUES (
    v_teacher_id,
    v_student_id,
    'enrollment_request',
    v_enrollment_id,
    v_subject_id
  );

  RETURN json_build_object('success', true, 'message', 'Enrollment request sent to teacher.');
END;
$$;

